### Page 9 of 33

```text
6

While the ultimate goal of this project is to provide the City of Cheyenne with up to three new Emergency
Response Facilities, the key to successfully achieving this will be how eﬀectively the design team can utilize the
potential sites available to the City.
From just our brief conversations with the attendees at the pre ‐proposal conference, it is evident that there are
several  concerns associated with the Dell Range site.  Most notably, the site is long and narrow and land‐locked
on all four sides by the Sam’s shopping center on the west, Wal‐ Mart on the north and east and Del Range Blvd.
to the south.  While the ﬁremen can control the traﬃc light in front of the station from their trucks to stop all
traﬃc on what is reportedly the busiest roadway in Wyoming, the positioning of the one story ﬁre house on the
southern portion of the site adds additional challenges for the ﬁremen when they return their ﬁre equipment to
the station.  Access to the ﬁre house by ﬁre equipment is made from Del Range on a dirt road that is located
along the west property line.  Fire equipment travels along this dirt road to the north of the site where they can re
‐position their vehicle and enter the ﬁre station equipment bays from the north.  Additionally we understand that
the location of utility boxes close to the station access routes have a tendency to test the driver’s ability to
maneuver the big rigs into the site.
Fortunately for the City of Cheyenne, Brian Harris of TCA wrote the book, or at least part of the book, on ﬁre
station site location and selection.  While the solution for the Dell Range facility will require a new site plan to
correct some of the issues we have been made aware of, Brian’s expertise in site evaluation will be invaluable to
the assessment of the other two sites.  As noted in his contribution to  SITE LOCATION AND SELECTION (co‐
authored by Eric Schaer & Forest Hooker ) from the  International Association of Fire Chief’s Fire Station:
Architectural Insight to Planning Design and Construction, questions that should be addressed during such an
assessment include:
Is space needed for training structures and drills?
Maximum crew parking required –what’s required at a shift change?
Is parking required for visiting apparatus?
What is the nature of service area: urban, suburban, rural?
The largest turning radius of apparatus to be used now or in the future will aﬀect paving layout.
project area 4
```

### Page 10 of 33

```text
7

On‐site utilities, fueling stations, back‐up generators.
Covered carports
Storm water retention or detentions requirements  –e.g. ponds or vaults
  Test‐to‐ﬁts of building and site needs determine minimum and maximum square footage needs of a site as well
as site access requirements –i.e. can a site be accessed from a single point or will multiple vehicle access points
produce a more eﬃcient use of site square footage. When looking at site needs, it is important to prioritize the
requirements and to distinguish between must‐haves and nice‐to‐haves. For instance, if a drive‐ through
apparatus bay is a must‐have the minimum site size will be much larger than if it fell into the nice‐
to‐have column. Well deﬁned and prioritized site needs provide a useful foundation for site
evaluation.

While it would be easy to say that Plan One/Architects and TCA Architecture are committed to the scope of work
outlined in the proposal and leave it at that; the statement would really fall short of our actual commitment to
the project.  The Plan One • TCA team is committed to provide the City of Cheyenne with a facility that melds
their vision of the facility with the most current ﬁre station design philosophies and principles ; meets the need
for replication at other sites; provides durable, ﬁreﬁghter friendly ﬁnishes; and is cognizant of budget.  We are
committed to being an active participant in the site assessment process, and perhaps most importantly, the
promotion of this project through the 1% tax initiative that will provide funding for construction.  Having
designed project in Wyoming for 30 years, Plan One truly understands that grass roots support for these types of
projects is key and is often the diﬀerence with this type of “make or break” funding situation.  Our ability to
provide photo realistic representations of the proposed facilities, plus our commitment to attend town hall
meetings to explain the philosophies involved in the programming and planning process will be aimed at
garnering support for the project.
While the nature of the facilities do add a degree of specialized programming to the project from both a
functional and prototypical standpoint, the basic tenents of design for both Plan One and TCA remain the same,
input from project stakeholders will make this project a success.  Both ﬁrms strongly adhere to the principle that
this facility needs to be designed for the City of Cheyenne and its current and future needs and, that what has
worked in other stations, might not be the right solution for Cheyenne.  In particular for this project we will work
together with the City to address speciﬁc “ﬁre facility issues” while developing the prototypical design requested
in the RFP.
Key to the operational layout of the facilities will be the incorporation of current and developing essential facility
standards, NFPA standards, trends in the ﬁre service relative to gender neutral facilities, development of eﬃcient
paths of response and certainly making sure the facility is durable and easy to maintain. Understanding how the
facilities are currently maintained will be key to increasing durability and “ﬁreﬁghter prooﬁng” the facility.
Another aspect of facility design which we recommend for review and consideration is the incorporation of
integrated training features to allow ﬁreﬁghters to have manipulative training opportunities on demand within
the facility itself. TCA has incorporated integrated training opportunities into many facilities at virtually no
additional cost by carefully designing features such as multipurpose spaces, repelling anchors, breach props,
conﬁned space props, knot tying bars, laddering walls etc. into the structure of the facility. Designing a forward
thinking facility which can accommodate not only today’s ﬁreﬁghters, but also the generations to come, will be
vital to the process.
