### Page 19 of 33

```text
16

Plan One / Architects is not aware of any potential conﬂicts of interest for our ﬁrm  or ,our consultants, which
would adversely aﬀect the City of Cheyenne and our ability to fulﬁll our obligations on this project in a manner
commensurate with expectations.

Design Philosophy
Both Plan One and TCA believe in a very open design philosophy.  While we are hired as design experts and bring
extensive knowledge to the table, we ﬁrmly believe that it is the stakeholders designated by the City and Fire
Department  who bring some of the most valuable information to the process.  They are the ones who know what
they would like to see in their facility, it is our job to bring as much of their vision to the project as possible based on
forward thinking design principles, eﬃcient layout, and an eye on budget.
We will kick‐oﬀ your project with a planning and scheduling workshop. This workshop will include
representatives of the City and Fire Department to review all of the issues, objectives, and requirements of your
projects. As a result of having a well established team experienced in ﬁre facility design, we understand the
timing and sequencing of design related issues and have the foresight to know when the appropriate questions
must be asked of both the client and one another to maintain eﬃciencies.
Through our unique programming methodology, we will then review how your stations will be used and what
your facilities will need to support, now and in the future. During this process decisions will be evaluated based
on operational eﬃciencies, durability, cost of maintenance and ownership, and energy reduction. All decisions
will be defensible and well‐documented.  Key to this eﬀort will be understanding your identiﬁed space needs to
determine what changes and improvements can be made to further reﬁne your eﬀorts. Our goal will be to plan
for ﬂexible and eﬃcient facilities designed to save you money in the years ahead. As the operations‐ based
program is veriﬁed, these program decisions will be used as a basis to evaluate prototype ﬁre station design
concepts. It is important to establish design criteria early on to assure budget and program alignment.
Understanding what works and does not work in your current stations, and others you have toured, will be key
to this process.
Site arrangement diagrams will demonstrate vehicular ﬂow including response, delivery vehicles, public/
employee parking, secure zones, general building arrangement and orientation. Each evaluation will also
determine the ideal building orientation for maximum energy eﬃciency design and develop a high performance
checklist for possible elements to consider for long‐term savings.
Concurrent with the development of your operational program, we will begin to evaluate site options in
preparation for a “test to ﬁt” process. This process is important in assuring that your site(s) is understood from a
land use, contextual and environmental perspective before conceptual design commences.
Following the programming and site analysis process we will develop conceptual concepts for the ﬁre station
based on speciﬁc circumstances of the site.
